## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of multi-limb bit tricks and how two's complement generalizes to 128 bits without branches.

## Key Concepts

- x & (-x) principle for lsb isolation across 2 limbs
- careful carry propagation from low to high limb
- avoiding branches by using arithmetic comparisons that yield 0/1

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 isolateLowestSetBit128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t loNeg = ~x.lo + 1;
  uint64_t carry = (loNeg < ~x.lo);
  uint64_t hiNeg = ~x.hi + carry;
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = x.lo & loNeg;
  r.hi = x.hi & hiNeg;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend this to also return the index of the isolated bit in a 0..127 range?
- What are the edge-case behaviors when x is zero or has a single 1 at the MSB?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of in-place bitboard style transforms, pure bitwise logic, and branchless design for a fixed-width data layout.

## Key Concepts
- Bit-slice permutation across 8x8 matrix
- Masked swaps and shifts to perform transposition in O(1)
- Endianness and layout nullified by a consistent row-major interpretation

## Code Example
```javascript
function transpose8x8(x) {
  // Use BigInt for 64-bit arithmetic in JS
  let t;
  const m1 = 0x5555555555555555n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 1n)) & m1;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 1n);

  const m2 = 0x3333333333333333n;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 2n)) & m2;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 2n);

  const m4 = 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  t = (x ^ (x >> 4n)) & m4;
  x = x ^ t ^ (t << 4n);

  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 16x16 on a 128-bit word? needed masks and shifts?
- How would you handle different input mappings (column-major) or endianness concerns?

